## Proof

**Interpretation of C-semantics.** We interpret "$\mathcal{X} \models_C \varphi$" as follows: $\mathcal{X}$ is a topological space equipped with a sheaf of rings $\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{X}}$ (or equivalently, $\mathcal{X}$ is a spectral space / spectrum of a ring with its structure sheaf), and $\varphi$ holds in the constructible/sheaf semantics — meaning $\varphi$ is satisfied at every point of $\mathcal{X}$ (in the stalkwise sense, where for each $x \in \mathcal{X}$, the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{X},x}$ classically satisfies $\varphi$). "C-satisfiable" means such a pair $(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{X}})$ exists. The cardinality $|\mathcal{X}|$ is the number of points of the topological space.

Under this interpretation, $\varphi$ is C-satisfiable if and only if $\varphi$ is classically satisfiable in some ring (take any stalk; conversely take a one-point space with a constant sheaf from a classical model). The critical question is whether we can always find a C-model on a **small topological space**.

